## React Re-renders: Causes and Prevention

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props change. Change is determined by `===` (reference equality), not deep equality. Primitives compare by value; objects and functions compare by identity.

**The trap:** every render creates new object/function instances, so these props always appear "changed":

```jsx
// Parent re-renders → new `style` object every time → Child always re-renders
function Parent() {
  return <Child style={{ color: 'red' }} onClick={() => doSomething()} />;
}
```

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

| Tool | What it memoizes | Use when |
|------|-----------------|----------|
| `React.memo` | A component's render output | Child is expensive and receives stable props |
| `useMemo` | A computed value | Computation is expensive or result is a reference passed as prop |
| `useCallback` | A function reference | Function is passed as a prop to a memoized child |

**Rule of thumb:** `useCallback` and `useMemo` are only useful when the consumer cares about reference stability. A memoized function passed to a non-memoized child saves nothing.

```jsx
// useCallback only matters because Child is wrapped in React.memo
const handler = useCallback(() => doSomething(id), [id]);
return <MemoizedChild onClick={handler} />;
```

**Don't over-memoize.** Memoization has its own cost (cache comparison). Use it where profiling shows a real problem.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Every consumer re-renders when the context value changes by reference — even if the data it cares about didn't change.

```jsx
// BAD: new object every render → all consumers re-render
function AuthProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <AuthContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      {children}
    </AuthContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

**Fix 1 — memoize the value:**
```jsx
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
```

**Fix 2 — split stable and unstable context** (more scalable):
```jsx
const UserContext = createContext(null);       // changes with user data
const UserActionsContext = createContext(null); // setUser never changes

function AuthProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  // setUser is stable; no need to memoize it
  return (
    <UserActionsContext.Provider value={setUser}>
      <UserContext.Provider value={user}>
        {children}
      </UserContext.Provider>
    </UserActionsContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

Components that only call `setUser` no longer re-render when `user` changes.

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — every keystroke re-renders `ExpensiveList`:

```jsx
function SearchPage() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');

  const filters = { active: true, category: 'all' }; // new ref every render
  const handleSelect = (item) => console.log(item);   // new ref every render

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList filters={filters} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}
```

**After** — `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `filters` or `handleSelect` actually change:

```jsx
// Wrap the child so React checks props before re-rendering
const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function ExpensiveList({ filters, onSelect }) {
  // ... expensive render
});

function SearchPage() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');

  // Stable references — recreated only when deps change
  const filters = useMemo(() => ({ active: true, category: 'all' }), []);
  const handleSelect = useCallback((item) => console.log(item), []);

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList filters={filters} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}
```

**Key insight:** `React.memo` on the child is the gate; `useMemo`/`useCallback` in the parent supply stable keys to pass through that gate. Neither works without the other.
